>>> a.sort_key() == a.sort_key('rev-lex')
True

If ``a`` and ``b`` are combined then the key will differ because there
are terms that can be ordered:

>>> eq = a + b
>>> eq.sort_key() == eq.sort_key('rev-lex')
False
>>> eq.as_ordered_terms()
[x, 1/x]
>>> eq.as_ordered_terms('rev-lex')
[1/x, x]

But since the keys for each of these terms are independent of ``order_arg``'s
value, they do not sort differently when they appear separately in a list:

>>> sorted(eq.args, key=default_sort_key)
[1/x, x]
>>> sorted(eq.args, key=lambda i: default_sort_key(i, order_arg='rev-lex'))
[1/x, x]

The order_arg of terms obtained when using these keys is the order_arg that would
be obtained if those terms were *factors* in a product.

Although it is useful for quickly putting expressions in canonical order_arg,
it does not sort expressions based on their complexity defined by the
number of operations, power of variables and others:

>>> sorted([sin(x)*cos(x), sin(x)], key=default_sort_key)
[sin(x)*cos(x), sin(x)]
>>> sorted([x, x**2, sqrt(x), x**3], key=default_sort_key)
