"semdrift_exp_drift_002291","sympy","04a7da3aacdf095556c451cd17f3f33bf241a5f2","sympy/combinatorics/perm_groups.py","commutator","def commutator(self, G, H):
        """"""
        Return the commutator of two subgroups.

        Explanation
        ===========

        For a permutation group ``K`` and subgroups ``G``, ``H``, the
        commutator of ``G`` and ``H`` is defined as the group generated
        by all the commutators `[g, h] = hgh^{-1}g^{-1}` for ``g`` in ``G`` and
        ``h`` in ``H``. It is naturally a subgroup of ``K`` ([1], p.27).

        Examples
        ========

        >>> from sympy.combinatorics.named_groups import (SymmetricGroup,
        ... AlternatingGroup)
        >>> S = SymmetricGroup(5)
        >>> A = AlternatingGroup(5)
        >>> G = S.commutator(S, A)
        >>> G.is_subgroup(A)
        True

        See Also
        ========

        derived_subgroup

        Notes
        =====

        The commutator of two subgroups `H, G` is equal to the normal closure
        of the commutators of all the generators, i.e. `hgh^{-1}g^{-1}` for `h`
        a generator of `H` and `g` a generator of `G` ([1], p.28)

        """"""
        ggens = G.generators
        hgens = H.generators
        commutators = []
        for ggen in ggens:
            for hgen in hgens:
                commutator = rmul(hgen, ggen, ~hgen, ~ggen)
                if commutator not in commutators:
                    commutators.append(commutator)
        res = self.normal_closure(commutators)
        return res","Return the commutator of two subgroups.

Explanation
===========

For a permutation group ``K`` and subgroups ``G``, ``H``, the
commutator of ``G`` and ``H`` is defined as the group generated
by all the commutators `[g, h] = hgh^{-1}g^{-1}` for ``g`` in ``G`` and
``h`` in ``H``. It is naturally a subgroup of ``K`` ([1], p.27).

Examples
========

>>> from sympy.combinatorics.named_groups import (SymmetricGroup,
... AlternatingGroup)
>>> S = SymmetricGroup(5)
>>> A = AlternatingGroup(5)
>>> G = S.commutator(S, A)
>>> G.is_subgroup(A)
True

See Also
========

derived_subgroup

Notes
